"Historical Timeline - Faroe Islands (https://www.faroeislands.fo/the-big-picture/history-of-the-faroe-islands/historical-timeline)\nciteturn707view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.faroeislands.fo/the-big-picture/history-of-the-faroe-islands/historical-timeline\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 54\nL0: [Input: Search here ...]\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†Home L3:   * cite1†The Big Picture L4:   * cite2†People & Society L5:   * cite3†Nature & Environment L6:   * cite4†Arts & Culture L7:   * cite5†Economy & Business L8:   * cite6†Government & Politics L9: \nL10: cite7†Back L11: \nL12: Historical Timeline\nL13: A Chronology of Key Events:\nL14: circa 600 AD - Irish monks settle on the islands.\nL15: circa 800 - Norwegian farmers arrive.\nL16: circa 1000 - Christianity introduced by order of the king of Norway.\nL17: 1035 - Faroe Islands become formally a land under the Kingdom of Norway (in reality the rule of Norwegian kings only secured by late 12th century).\nL18: 1380 - As a land under the Norwegian Crown the Faroe Islands enter together with Norway into a Dual Monarchy with the Kingdom of Denmark.\nL19: 1655 - Faroe Islands are presented to the von Gabel family. Their oppressive feudal rule ends in 1709 when the Faroes come under direct Danish rule.\nL20: 1814 - With the Kiel Treaty of 1814 Norway is separated from the union with Denmark. The Faroes are maintained under the rule of the Kingdom of Denmark.\nL21: 1816 - Faroe Islands status as county of the Kingdom of Denmark.\nL22: 1850 - The Danish constitution is by Danish order extended to the Faroe Islands, which are given representation in both houses of the Danish parliament.\nL23: 1856 - Danish Royal trade monopoly abolished. Free trade is established.\nL24: 1872 - Purchase of the first seafaring fishing vessel heralds the growth of the islands' main economic activity.\nL25: 1906-09 - First political parties are formed, representing divisions over the self-government issue.\nL26: 1940 - World War II: British forces occupy the Faroes while Denmark is under German occupation. The Faroese flag is recognised by the British Government.\nL27: 1946 - Narrow vote for secession from Denmark in referendum. Danish king dissolves Faroese parliament.\nL28: 1948 - Home rule act is passed. The islands become an autonomous, self-governing nation under the external sovereignty of the Kingdom of Denmark.\nL29: 1973 - Faroese parliament decides not to join the European Community (now European Union) with Denmark.\nL30: early 1990s - Severe economic crisis, amid falling fish prices and depleted stocks. Unemployment and emigration rise. Almost 10 % decline in population.\nL31: 1998 - After the General Election a pro-independece Government coalition is formed under Prime Minister Mr Anfinn Kallsberg (People's Party).\nL32: 2000 - Negotiations on Faroese sovereignty between Faroese and Danish governments commence and fail.\nL33: 2001 March - Planned referendum on independence is cancelled after Denmark says a pro-independence result would lead to a very sudden halt in Danish subsidies.\nL34: 2002 January - Faroes take over responsibility of the entire education system and a major part of social security, resulting in a 35 per cent reduction in annual Danish State subsidies.\nL35: 2002 April - After the General Election a new Government coalition is formed consisting of both independence and unionist parties. A more gradual self-government policy is adopted.\nL36: 2003 December - Pro-independence party pulls out of coalition.\nL37: 2004 - After early elections in January a three-party coalition is formed with Mr Jóannes Eidesgaard (Social Democrat) as Prime Minister.\nL38: 2005 - After negotiations with Denmark, Faroese self-government is widened with greater powers to unilaterally take over new government areas and also to act in foreign policy matters.\nL39: 2008 January - Parliamentary elections. New governing coalition formed. Joannes Eidesgaard continues as Prime Minister.\nL40: 2008 September - Mr Kaj Leo Holm Johannesen (Union Party) becomes prime minister and forms new coalition government after the collapse of the coalition led by Mr Joannes Eidesgaard.\nL41: 2011 October - Centre right Union and People's parties increase their share of the vote in parliamentary elections. Mr Kaj Leo Holm Johannesen continues as Prime Minister.\nL42: 2015 September - New Government takes office in the Faroes. Prime Minister, Mr Aksel Johannesen’s Social Democratic Party (Javnaðarflokkurin) formes a coalition with the Republican Party (Tjóðveldi) and the Progressive Party (Framsókn).\nL43: The new Government consists of 8 ministers, including the Prime Minister, with four men and four women.\nL44: Tinganes, Postrúm 64\nL45: FO-110 Tórshavn\nL46: faroeislands.fo@tinganes.fo\nL47: \nL48: Useful links\nL49: \nL50: cite8†Føroya landsstýri - Government of the Faroe Islands†www.government.fo cite9†Løgtingið - the Faroese Parliament†www.logting.fo cite10†Visit Faroe Islands†visitfaroeislands.com cite11†Fróðskaparsetur Føroya - University of the Faroe Islands†www.setur.fo cite12†Vinnuhúsið - House of Industry†www.industry.fo L51: \nL52: Copyright © 2026 Føroya landsstýri. All rights reserved.\nL53: Ment, forritað og sniðgivið hava cite13†Sansir†www.sansir.fo "
